<section class="excard" id="ex-a" aria-labelledby="ex-a-h"><h3 id="ex-a-h">Example A · Path A, from the trail How fast will you spin?</h3><dl><dt>What I want to understand:</dt><dd data-fill="understand">how spinning objects behave differently from objects moving in a straight line, and how a spin changes when the mass is rearranged.</dd><dt>Given</dt><dd data-fill="given">how fast something is spinning, in radians per second, and how its mass is arranged around the axis before and after the arrangement changes, as a moment of inertia in kilogram square meters,</dd><dt>I will be able to predict</dt><dd data-fill="predict">how fast it will spin afterwards, in radians per second, as long as nothing outside twists it while the mass moves.</dd><dt>Predicting it is useful because</dt><dd data-fill="useful">the same relationship explains how skaters, divers and satellites control their spin, and my phone's gyroscope lets me check the prediction on a spinning chair.</dd></dl></section>
<section class="excard" id="ex-b" aria-labelledby="ex-b-h"><h3 id="ex-b-h">Example B · Path B, from "why a swing feels the same however high you go"</h3><dl><dt>What I want to understand:</dt><dd>what sets how long one swing of a playground swing takes.</dd><dt>Given</dt><dd>the length of the swing's chains in meters, and the strength of gravity, 9.8 m/s&sup2;,</dd><dt>I will be able to predict</dt><dd>the time for one full swing in seconds, as long as the swing moves through a small angle, less than about 15 degrees from straight down.</dd><dt>Predicting it is useful because</dt><dd>it explains why a pendulum clock keeps time, and why pushing a child higher does not make each swing take much longer.</dd></dl></section>
<section class="excard" id="ex-c" aria-labelledby="ex-c-h"><h3 id="ex-c-h">Example C · Path C, a project in Tinkercad Circuits</h3><dl><dt>What I want to understand:</dt><dd>how the resistor I choose sets the brightness of an LED in a circuit I build.</dd><dt>Given</dt><dd>the battery voltage in volts, the LED's forward voltage in volts, and the resistor's value in ohms,</dd><dt>I will be able to predict</dt><dd>the current through the LED in milliamperes, as long as the battery's voltage stays steady and the LED is lit.</dd><dt>Predicting it is useful because</dt><dd>it tells me which resistor keeps an LED from burning out, which is the first thing anyone wiring an Arduino project needs to know.</dd></dl></section>
